#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""🧩 make-themes — turns the Ghostty themes into Zed and VS Code themes, same names and colors.

Every file in ghostty/themes/ becomes one editor theme, so run this again after those change:

    editors/zed/Neon.json, Solid.json, Monkeytype.json    Zed theme families
    editors/kronikus-themes.vsix                          a VS Code extension with all of them

Ghostty gives 16 terminal colors and a few more. An editor needs roles, so:

    editor text      foreground
    keywords         palette 5, the accent of the whole setup
    strings          palette 2
    numbers          palette 3
    functions, tags  palette 4
    types            palette 6
    comments         palette 8, the quiet color
    operators        palette 7
    errors           palette 1, and nothing else is red, so red always means trouble

A terminal can show its accent at 3:1 because it's only used for marks, but code is read all day:
syntax colors are lifted to 4.5:1 if they're fainter (same hue, the UI keeps the exact accent),
and the made ones turn out of the way if that brings two together.
A theme with an accent as pale as its text gets bold keywords instead.

The screens follow the look of the tmux bar: flat, one background, the accent only on what's
active, the quiet color on the rest. Integrated terminals use the 16 colors unchanged.
"""
import importlib.machinery, io, json, math, os, re, sys, zipfile

sys.dont_write_bytecode = True
